#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "81d5fa54-0b80-52c1-97d7-6f8fb231f692" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "8c2dba74-7d6e-5074-947e-907464ee59c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-211" :question/problem "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t) = 3t^2 +3t +1$. Calculate the velocity at time $t$." :question/worked-solution "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(3t^{2} + 3t + 1) \\\\\n&= 6t + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063928 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1ec1379-ce50-53c7-b04d-3380676ebeb9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/id #uuid "ede4e9d4-ae2e-5448-9e2d-3f6e93761d8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-212" :question/problem "The position $x$, measured in meters, of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t) = 6t^4 -3t^2 +t$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the velocity at time $t=1$." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(6t^{4} - 3t^{2} + t) \\\\\n&= 24t^{3} - 6t + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=1$, we substitute $t=1$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(1) &= 24(1)^{3} - 6(1) + 1 \\\\\n&= 24 - 6 + 1 \\\\\n&= 19\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle is $19\\,\\textrm{m/s}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063929 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b380ed-1974-5876-8c88-a45773741b48" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/id #uuid "36167a6f-27ba-503a-a1a8-8485f021d167" :question/math-academy-id "e-214" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by\n$s(t) = t^2 - 36t,\\quad t\\geq 0$.\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{ds}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - 36t) \\\\\n&= 2t - 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=1$, the velocity is\n\n$$\nv(1) = 2(1) - 36 = -34\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is\n\n$$\n\\mid {-34} \\mid = 34\\, \\textrm{m/s}\n$$"}]}
